14:29:48 | INFO | 🤖 Target: It is completely understandable why it feels like common sense! Because sleep is a state of unconsciousness, it is very easy to assume our senses just shut down completely. It is a widely held belief, and for the most part, our brains do an excellent job of making it *feel* true by blocking out minor aches and pains so we can rest. But if you think about a few common, real-life experiences, you might realize you've actually witnessed the body feeling pain during sleep: * **The "Charlie Horse":** Have you ever been jolted awake in the middle of the night by a sudden, agonizing calf cramp? * **Being Sick:** If you've ever had a terrible earache, toothache, or stomach bug, you know how hard it is to stay asleep. The pain keeps waking you up. * **Sleeping on an Injury:** If you have a bruised shoulder and roll onto it in your sleep, the sudden spike in pain will usually wake you up immediately so you can roll back over. If our bodies truly couldn't feel pain while asleep, we would sleep peacefully right through a severe muscle cramp or a throbbing tooth. The reason "common sense" tells us we don't feel pain is because of how memory and consciousness work. When you are awake, you are consciously aware of every little ache, itch, and pain. When you are asleep, your brain puts those minor sensations on "autopilot." You might toss, turn, or shift your weight to relieve a minor ache, but because you don't wake up, **you don't remember it**. You only become consciously aware of the pain when it crosses a certain threshold and forces you to wake up. So, while it definitely *feels* like we power down completely at night, biology keeps our pain receptors acting as a built-in alarm system. It's an evolutionary survival mechanism—if an early human was bitten by an insect or animal while sleeping, they needed to be able to feel it and wake up instantly to protect themselves!
